Table: Users

+---------------+---------+
| Column Name   | Type    |
+---------------+---------+
| user_id       | int     |
| name          | varchar |
| mail          | varchar |
+---------------+---------+
user_id is the primary key (column with unique values) for this table.
This table contains information of the users signed up in a website. Some e-mails are invalid.

Write a solution to find the users who have valid emails.
A valid e-mail has a prefix name and a domain where:
    The prefix name is a string that may contain letters (upper or lower case), digits, underscore '_', period '.', and/or dash '-'. The prefix name must start with a letter.
    The domain is '@leetcode.com'.

Approach:
The prefix must start with a letter, can contain letters/digist/underscore'_'/period'.'/dash'-' 
must end with @leetcode.com 

using (~)
^: ensures pattern starts at beginning
[a-zA-Z]: requires first character must to be letter
[a-zA-Z0-9_.-]: allow leeter, digits, underscore, period, dash
@leetcode\.com: matches the requires domain
$: ensures pattern ends here


-- Write your PostgreSQL query statement below
select *
from users
where mail ~ '^[a-zA-Z]+[a-zA-Z0-9_.-]*@leetcode\.com$'